# Auth capability

Scaffolding-instruction text and auth-specific test-plan text for every framework family, branched on `auth.method` (`"jwt"` vs `"apikey"`), plus two shared framework-agnostic JWT sub-features: refresh tokens and RBAC. One function pair per framework family; Flask and Bottle already share one test-plan function, since their shapes are near-identical.

| Framework family | Scaffold builder | Test-plan builder |
|---|---|---|
| FastAPI (reused by Python brownfield) | `_build_auth_step_block` | `_auth_test_plan` |
| Django | `_build_django_auth_step_block` | `_django_auth_test_plan` |
| Tornado | `_build_tornado_auth_step_block` | `_tornado_auth_test_plan` |
| Flask | `_build_flask_auth_blocks` | `_flask_bottle_auth_test_plan` (shared with Bottle) |
| Bottle | `_build_bottle_auth_blocks` | `_flask_bottle_auth_test_plan` (shared with Flask) |
| Node.js/Express | `_build_nodejs_auth_blocks` | `_nodejs_auth_test_plan` |
| Java monolith | `_build_java_auth_step_block` | Java JUnit/curl helpers, shared with microservices |
| Java microservices | `_build_java_microservices_auth_step_block` | Same two Java helpers |

Java brownfield surfaces auth as its own display section (a "REQUESTED CONFIG TARGETS" line plus a standalone "AUTH TARGET" block); Python and Node.js brownfield only steer test-plan wording.

## What gets scaffolded

- **apikey** (all 8 families): an `ApiKey` model/entity (hashed key, unique+indexed, name, active flag, timestamps; Java adds an expiry), a raw-key-hashing helper, a key-generation helper, a create/list/revoke management surface.
- **JWT** (all 8 families): bcrypt password hashing, a generated secret written to the env file, never hardcoded.
- **Refresh tokens** (`auth.jwt.refresh=True`): a distinct refresh vs. access claim, a separate longer expiry, a persisted+revocable refresh-token row, `/auth/refresh` and `/auth/logout` (logout must never touch the still-valid access token). Expiry comparisons use timezone-aware datetimes with a few seconds of clock-skew leeway.
- **RBAC** (`auth.jwt.rbac=True`, or `auth.api_key.rbac=True` for Java apikey): a role field, baked into the token claim or resolved at key-lookup time; one reusable enforcement primitive returning 403 (never 401) on mismatch; a role is immutable once assigned. Spring needs a `"ROLE_"`-prefixed authority or every role gets 403 on every endpoint.

## Admin-key protection scheme (apikey mode only)

Every framework protects its own create/list/revoke `/api-keys` surface with a **separate admin credential** — those endpoints are never left open to any caller. One shared shape across all 8 families:

> A separate `X-Admin-Key` header is checked, using a constant-time comparison, against an `ADMIN_API_KEY` value read from the environment. A mismatch or missing header is a 401 (not a redirect, not a 403). This gate protects key management ONLY — it must never guard business endpoints.

Only the comparison primitive and read syntax vary by language:

| Family | Compare primitive | Wiring |
|---|---|---|
| FastAPI | `secrets.compare_digest` | `Depends(get_admin_key)` |
| Django | `secrets.compare_digest` | `IsAdminApiKey(BasePermission)`, applied to the ApiKey viewset only |
| Tornado | `secrets.compare_digest` | `get_admin_key(handler)`, called first in every `ApiKeyHandler` method |
| Flask | `secrets.compare_digest` | `@require_admin_key` decorator |
| Bottle | `secrets.compare_digest` | `require_admin_key` decorator |
| Node.js | `crypto.timingSafeEqual` (both sides sha256-hashed first, to avoid a length-mismatch throw on raw values) | `adminAuth` middleware |
| Java monolith | `MessageDigest.isEqual` | Checked first in every `ApiKeyController` method |
| Java microservices | `MessageDigest.isEqual`, key generated per service, exported as a real env var — never written into that service's properties file | Same as monolith |

Java's Spring `SecurityFilterChain` wiring: `.requestMatchers("/api/v1/api-keys/**", "/actuator/health", "/docs/**", ...).permitAll().anyRequest().authenticated()` then `.addFilterBefore(apiKeyFilter, ...)`. Microservices warns explicitly against a second `CorsConfig` bean — it doubles the `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header, which browsers reject outright.

## Test plans

One shared narrative across all 8 families, only the tool syntax differs:

- **apikey**: admin creates a throwaway key via the admin header, then the raw key is sent as the *business* header (never `Authorization: Bearer`, never `X-Admin-Key`) on entity CRUD; also verify a missing/wrong header returns 401.
- **JWT**: register → login → capture token → CRUD with the token.

Tool delta: curl script, pytest/test-client fixture, Tavern YAML, or (Java) JUnit integration-test additions + curl lines — same narrative, different syntax. FastAPI additionally gets Schemathesis, with a session-scoped fixture per method.

## Gating

- `auth.method`: `"jwt"` (default) vs `"apikey"` selects the whole branch.
- `auth.jwt.refresh` / `auth.jwt.rbac` / `auth.api_key.rbac` (Java apikey only): independent feature gates.
- `auth.api_key.header_name`: the **business-endpoint** header name is configurable; `X-Admin-Key` is always the literal string, never configurable.
- `auth.api_key.expiry_minutes` (Java only): an expired key is treated identically to an inactive one — no distinct HTTP status.

## Greenfield vs. brownfield

- **Greenfield**: scaffolds new auth from scratch, per the tables above.
- **Brownfield** (all three languages): never scaffolds new auth. `auth.method` only picks which test-plan wording to write for whatever auth the cloned repo already has, and for Java is also displayed as its own config-target section.
