2026-09-28 10:05:33,412 INFO deepagent.transcript: [ai] {"project_name":"job-d559a533-f213-4744-9e90-c578859a7a58","task_prompt":"Build the project named `job-d559a533-f213-4744-9e90-c578859a7a58` as a greenfield Java monolith REST backend for enterprise HR management.\n\nBefore implementing anything, read these instructions:\n- `/aisdlc/modes/greenfield/07-java-monolith.md`\n- `/aisdlc/capabilities/auth.md`\n- `/aisdlc/capabilities/build-quality-and-testing.md`\n- `/aisdlc/capabilities/reports-and-deploy.md`\n\n## Fixed technology stack\n- Language: Java 21.0.12.1\n- Framework: Spring Boot 3.4.0\n- Build tool: Gradle 8.11.1\n- ORM: Spring Data JPA with Hibernate 6.6.2\n- Database: MySQL 8.4.3\n- Messaging: none requested\n- Authentication: JWT bearer tokens with BCrypt password hashing\n- Authorization: exactly the `HR`, `MANAGER`, and `EMPLOYEE` roles; use Spring Security `ROLE_` authorities\n- API conventions: JSON REST APIs under `/api`, validated request bodies, appropriate HTTP status codes, and structured JSON errors for validation, missing-resource, authentication, authorization, and business-rule failures\n- Infrastructure to generate: externalized MySQL, JPA, JWT, and server configuration in `application.yml`; do not invent additional broker, cache, or messaging infrastructure\n- Testing framework: curl-based API verification, the documented Java monolith default; also configure Spring Boot test dependencies and JUnit Platform as required by the build\n\nCreate a standard Gradle Spring Boot application using package `com.enterprise.hr`. Keep password hashes out of every API response. Do not add an ADMIN role or any roles beyond the three specified below.\n\n## Functional specification\n\n### 1. Domain entities and workflow enums\nImplement these persistable JPA entities with database-generated `Long` identifiers, required-field constraints, and the stated relationships:\n\n- `Employee` in table `employee`: required unique `employeeCode`, required `fullName`, required unique `email`, required BCrypt `passwordHash`, required `Role`, and required integer `annualLeaveEntitlementDays`. It may optionally reference a `Department`, a `Designation`, and another `Employee` as its direct manager. Model the self-referencing hierarchy so an employee can have many direct subordinates. An employee owns attendance records, leave requests, and payroll records. Never serialize `passwordHash`.\n- `Department` in table `department`: required unique `name` and optional `description`; one department may be assigned to many employees.\n- `Designation` in table `designation`: required unique `name` and optional `description`; one designation may be assigned to many employees.\n- `AttendanceRecord` in table `attendance`: required employee, server-recorded `Instant checkInAt`, and nullable server-recorded `Instant checkOutAt` while open.\n- `LeaveRequest` in table `leave_request`: required employee, inclusive `LocalDate startDate` and `endDate`, required `reason`, required `LeaveStatus`, and optional reviewing employee and decision `Instant`.\n- `PayrollRecord` in table `payroll_record`: required employee, `LocalDate payPeriodStart` and `payPeriodEnd`, required `BigDecimal grossAmount`, `deductionAmount`, and `netAmount`, plus optional `Instant paidAt`.\n- `PerformanceReviewCycle` in table `performance_review_cycle`: required unique `name`, required start and end `LocalDate` values, and optional `description`.\n\nExpose the exact enum values `Role.HR`, `Role.MANAGER`, `Role.EMPLOYEE` and `LeaveStatus.PENDING`, `LeaveStatus.APPROVED`, `LeaveStatus.REJECTED`. Provide JPA constructors and service-compatible getters/setters. Enforce uniqueness and required values with appropriate JPA/database constraints.\n\n### 2. Authentication and authorization\nImplement public `POST /api/auth/login`, accepting employee email and password and returning a JWT for valid credentials. Use BCrypt for password verification and onboarding password encoding. The JWT subject must be the employee email and it must carry the employee role authority.\n\nConfigure stateless Spring Security with a JWT request filter. Only `POST /api/auth/login` is anonymous; all other routes require authentication. Invalid or missing bearer tokens return structured 401 errors. Authorization failures return structured 403 errors. Enforce HR-only, manager-only, and authenticated-user routes as specified below, using route security and/or `@PreAuthorize`.\n\n### 3. HR employee onboarding and hierarchy\nImplement:\n- `POST /api/employees`: HR-only onboarding with credentials, role, annual leave entitlement, and optional department and designation assignments. Store only a BCrypt password hash.\n- `PUT /api/employees/{employeeId}/manager`: HR-only assignment or clearing of the employee’s direct manager. The manager must be another employee when assigned.\n\nValidate all request bodies and return appropriate errors for duplicate identities, missing referenced resources, invalid assignments, and other business-rule violations.\n\n### 4. Department and designation management\nImplement department routes:\n- `POST /api/departments`: HR-only creation.\n- `GET /api/departments`: available to any authenticated role.\n- `PUT /api/departments/{id}`: HR-only update.\n- `DELETE /api/departments/{id}`: HR-only deletion, rejected as a business-rule conflict if employees are assigned to it.\n\nImplement designation routes:\n- `POST /api/designations`: HR-only creation.\n- `GET /api/designations`: available to any authenticated role.\n- `PUT /api/designations/{id}`: HR-only update.\n- `DELETE /api/designations/{id}`: HR-only deletion, rejected as a business-rule conflict if employees are assigned to it.\n\n### 5. Attendance\nImplement authenticated employee attendance operations:\n- `POST /api/attendance/check-in`: create an open attendance record for the caller using the current server `Instant`.\n- `POST /api/attendance/check-out`: close the caller’s open attendance record using the current server `Instant`.\n- `GET /api/attendance/me`: list the caller’s attendance records.\n\nDo not allow a second open attendance record before checkout. Enforce valid open/closed workflow state and return structured business-rule errors for invalid check-in or check-out operations.\n\n### 6. Leave requests and approval workflow\nImplement:\n- `POST /api/leave-requests`: any authenticated employee creates a pending request with inclusive start and end dates and a required reason.\n- `GET /api/leave-requests/me`: any authenticated employee lists only their own requests.\n- `GET /api/leave-requests/pending-subordinates`: a manager lists pending requests from direct subordinates only.\n- `PATCH /api/leave-requests/{id}/decision`: a manager directly assigned to the requesting employee approves or rejects a pending request. The decision input may contain only `APPROVED` or `REJECTED`, never `PENDING`.\n\nOnly direct managers may decide subordinate requests. Only pending requests are decidable. On a decision, record the reviewing manager and decision timestamp. Approval must reject overlapping approved requests and must reject requests whose inclusive duration exceeds the employee’s remaining annual leave balance. Calculate inclusive duration from start date through end date. Remaining leave equals the employee’s annual entitlement minus approved leave days. Support the `PENDING`, `APPROVED`, and `REJECTED` statuses exactly.\n\n### 7. Payroll management\nImplement HR-only payroll routes:\n- `POST /api/payroll-records`: create a payroll record.\n- `GET /api/payroll-records`: list payroll records.\n- `PUT /api/payroll-records/{id}`: update a payroll record.\n- `DELETE /api/payroll-records/{id}`: delete a payroll record.\n\nValidate referenced employees, dates, monetary fields, and resource identifiers. Return 404 for missing resources and structured business-rule errors for invalid payroll state or values.\n\n### 8. Performance review cycles\nImplement:\n- `POST /api/performance-review-cycles`: HR-only creation.\n- `GET /api/performance-review-cycles`: any authenticated role lists cycles.\n- `PUT /api/performance-review-cycles/{id}`: HR-only update.\n- `DELETE /api/performance-review-cycles/{id}`: HR-only deletion.\n\nValidate required fields, unique cycle names, valid date ranges, and missing-resource behavior.\n\n### 9. Administrative reports\nTreat HR as the administrative role because no separate ADMIN role was requested. Implement HR-only:\n- `GET /api/reports/attendance`: return attendance report data, optionally filtered by inclusive date range.\n- `GET /api/reports/leave-balances`: return every employee’s annual leave entitlement, approved leave days, and remaining leave balance.\n\nLeave-balance calculations must use inclusive approved leave days and compute remaining balance as entitlement minus approved days. Validate optional report date ranges and return structured errors for invalid filters.\n\n### 10. DTOs, repositories, errors, and HTTP behavior\nCreate typed validated request and response DTOs for every mutating route, login/token responses, all entity responses, attendance and leave workflows, payroll, review cycles, reports, and API errors. DTOs must never expose employee password hashes.\n\nCreate Spring Data repositories for all seven entities. Include repository methods for employee lookup by email and employee code, attendance records and open-record lookup, employee attendance reporting, employee leave requests, pending direct-subordinate requests, approved leave totals and overlap checks, leave-balance reporting, duplicate checks, and normal CRUD operations. Controllers must not access repositories directly.\n\nImplement a `@RestControllerAdvice` with public `ResourceNotFoundException` and `BusinessRuleException` types. Map validation failures to 400, missing resources to 404, business-rule conflicts to 409, authentication failures to 401, and authorization failures to 403. Responses must use one consistent structured JSON API error shape with no stack traces or password data.\n\n### 11. Configuration, build, and verification\nCreate:\n- `settings.gradle` with the Gradle root project name `hr-management-api`.\n- `build.gradle` applying Spring Boot 3.4.0 and dependency management, configuring Java 21 and Gradle 8.11.1 compatibility, and including Spring Web, Validation, Security, Spring Data JPA, MySQL Connector/J, JJWT API/implementation/Jackson modules, and Spring Boot test dependencies with JUnit Platform.\n- `src/main/java/com/enterprise/hr/HrManagementApplication.java` with a standard `@SpringBootApplication` entry point.\n- `src/main/resources/application.yml` with datasource URL, username, and password sourced from environment variables with local MySQL defaults; Hibernate schema update and MySQL dialect; configurable JWT secret and expiration; and suitable JSON error configuration. Do not hard-code production credentials.\n\nImplement the project files in the declared waves and package locations where practical, including `HrDomain.java`, `HrDtos.java`, `HrRepositories.java`, `ApiExceptionHandler.java`, `JwtSecurity.java`, `HrService.java`, and `HrController.java`. Generate clean, multi-line, properly formatted source files.\n\nRun and fix:\n- `./gradlew build`\n- `./gradlew test`\n- An API verification request such as `curl -sS -X POST http://localhost:8080/api/auth/login -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{\"email\":\"unknown@example.com\",\"password\":\"invalid\"}'`\n\nFollow the Java monolith mode’s boot verification, testing, report, and deployment-artifact instructions. Do not use Kafka, pagination, external integrations, or push workflows because they are not part of this plan."}
